    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Following up on that, how do courts typically handle the \"mitigation\" aspect? If the tenant claims the disruption was due to external chaos or systemic breakdown, what kind of evidence are they usually expected to provide to prove they actually tried to minimize the impact on the business before invoking the clause?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In Singapore courts, courts typically require tenants to demonstrate that they made reasonable efforts to mitigate the impact of the force majeure event before invoking its protection under the clause. This may involve providing evidence such as records of communication with landlords, suppliers, or other parties, as well as internal measures taken to minimize losses, such as reducing staff, implementing cost-cutting measures, or relocating operations temporarily. The level of detail and effort expected will vary depending on the specific circumstances, but generally, courts will consider whether the tenant acted reasonably and in good faith to mitigate the consequences of the force majeure event.",
      "source": "target",
